    {
      "dom_id": "question-64935",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "$\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=$",
      "worked_solution": "Let's remind ourselves of the special trigonometric ratios, as given by the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14256898/assets/dbf36192ac332dc49ed75278838e989a37ac7d888311be1b8b059a7b59667aa6.png)\n\nFrom the unit circle, we see that the angle $\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ corresponds to the point $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}).$\n\n- Since the cosine of the angle corresponds to the $x$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\n- Since the sine of the angle corresponds to the $y$-coordinate, we have $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.$\n\nTherefore, using the fact that $\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta },$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{tan}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})}=\\frac{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}{(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})}=1.\n$$",
